7592186045417] [17592186179714 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bd5c5fd8-409c-5b92-a0f1-4466c76cdbe0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179719 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179719 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186179966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179719 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186179973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179719 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179719 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179719 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179719 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179719 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179719 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179719 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179719 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179719 #edb/ref 1039 "Sketching the Gradient Vector Field of a Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179719 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179719 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7a43f82e-6682-56af-994a-b0bdf12b4095" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179846 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179846 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179846 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179846 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179846 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179846 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179846 #edb/ref 1045 "Gradient Vector Fields" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179846 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186180447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179846 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179846 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "563e73f4-5c65-5c01-b1d4-1909214d3b23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179967 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179967 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122413" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179967 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/11/11c759098d959c7dbdcb81686640bfc6a01d4dc0f9b12e6ff45f3028ddc4d2db.png)\n\nThe gradient vector field of the function $f(x, y)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f(x, y)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179967 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "43a11e4f-11c8-502a-b54b-73b03e51f3ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179973 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179973 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186387298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179973 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122789" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179973 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179973 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179973 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fe3ebbef-9b0f-50cb-ab18-6df5f7b1d83c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179973 #edb/ref 1074 "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}\\,i-y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,2),$ $(2,-3),$ and $(2,1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,2) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot (-2)^{2}\\,i-2\\,j=3\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-3) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\,i-(-3)\\,j=3\\,i+3\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\,i-\\,j=3\\,i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/26/26a5c0123d93231a800456e9f06f6ba7450861838c87d4d1b3ae77dab4cec6cf.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180447 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180447 #edb/ref 1061 35370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180447 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "42fa31ed-8f97-5aa3-89e8-9d271c417891" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180447 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180448 #edb/ref 1061 35372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180448 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "93326d12-a17a-5177-8940-426e5dd661b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180448 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180452 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180452 #edb/ref 1061 35387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180452 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cc32145d-a804-5c13-ba20-8531d24745d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180452 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180458 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180458 #edb/ref 1061 35460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180458 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f3f39866-fb9c-5fab-80a0-21104a970e7c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180458 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180460 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180460 #edb/ref 1061 35468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180460 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7dce8a1b-d2dc-5ba7-9445-52bd966f8de5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180460 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180700 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that  if $f(x,y)$ is a function whose partial derivatives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y},\n$$\n\nexist and are continuous at a point $(x_0,y_0) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ then the gradient of $f$ evaluated at $(x_0, y_0)$ is given by\n\n \n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0) = \\dfrac{\\partial f }{\\partial x}(x_0, y_0) \\,\\mathbf{i} + \\dfrac{\\partial f }{\\partial y} (x_0, y_0)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\, .\n$$\n\n \n\nNow suppose that the gradient vector exists at every point $(x,y)$ contained within the set $U\\subset \\mathbb R^2.$ Then, the **gradient vector field** of $f$ is defined as the map\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\longmapsto \\nabla f(x,y), \\qquad (x,y)\\in U.\n$$\n\nAs an example, consider the function $f(x,y)=x^2-y^2.$ To find the gradient vector field of $f,$ we proceed as follows.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =2x\\,i-2y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA sketch of this vector field is shown below.\n\n![](images/74/7437bd110810a0356a25afea897634c152b0441aee8e6403aa051dd2c1f205a7.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180700 #edb/ref 1065 5764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180700 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fe62959b-3e46-5af2-ba87-08f376c38b4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180700 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180705 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = x^2+y,$ shown below. How is this vector field related to the level curves of $f?$\n\n![](images/ef/ef5800723603b78beeb40c4352153f37298d4836905a6d8b751a919924664fa5.png)\n\nTo answer this question, we note the following:\n\n- As we've seen, the gradient of $f$ at a particular point $(x_0, y_0)$ lies *perpendicular* to the level curve $f(x,y) = f(x_0, y_0).$\n\n- Therefore, the gradient field vectors of $f$ at an arbitrary point $(x,y)$ lie perpendicular to its level curve at $(x,y).$\n\n- Moreover, the vectors always point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ is increasing.\n\nNow, if we superimpose the level curves of $f$ onto its vector field, we get a picture that looks as follows:\n\n![](images/5b/5bd2729b82312a50ee0c274fc1591545588f4881e2e279e80aca6b68f76ee824.png)\n\nNotice that the field vectors are indeed perpendicular to the level curves.\n\nWe can use a similar idea to sketch the vector field of a function using its level curves. Let's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180705 #edb/ref 1065 5774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180705 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "60bb3f88-e435-5258-8b56-9c92481e7976" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180705 #edb/ref 1067 "The Relationship Between the Vector Field of a Function and Its Contour Plot" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253671 #edb/ref 1080 "images/8b/8bd5b7575d1693935df0d25a259767f0dd267fc1153139cd55cebad75e3988fd.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253671 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253671 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "00678095-4785-5a48-bfd2-04906406b23d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253699 #edb/ref 1080 "images/4c/4c1a0035b37bc0137dc2ac164c980d538f9362feb3b439e663885f8aa7178124.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253699 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253699 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03aaca50-6ba1-5767-8a1d-61289921be5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253939 #edb/ref 1080 "images/d3/d3fc823cfcea400219e553f9e6911a312f0a14c7f39aafb49d1af3463cb3e46b.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253939 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253939 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1f1e18b0-1117-563b-8441-3490065e61e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254269 #edb/ref 1080 "images/0e/0ea540eb4a48d1fb1777e2d6397e6d38937e9795c4d810b48c0fda0801d1c9ba.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254269 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254269 #edb/re