17592186045417] [17592186255938 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186255938 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186255365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186255938 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0e297b46-c5d5-5311-943f-eb3962e376bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256221 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186253671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256221 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186253699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256221 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186254452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256221 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186254671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256221 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186255043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256221 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256221 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256221 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186253671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256221 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a46a2d28-3dee-5856-b9b2-7ac4fb2b706b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256366 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186254314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256366 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186254930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256366 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186255484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256366 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186255617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256366 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186255684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256366 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256366 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256366 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186254930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256366 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fefeda5b-1048-597b-a49a-96c4ffaa9319" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256708 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256708 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122738" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256708 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256708 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{(y-2x)^{2}}{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256708 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7de89f2b-b3a4-52f5-bd83-f755a3097808" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256708 #edb/ref 1074 "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{(y-2x)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2(y-2x) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{(y-2x)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =y-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =-2(y-2x)\\,i+(y-2x)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(1,1),$ $(1,3),$ and $(-2,-3).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(1,1) & =-2\\cdot (1-2\\cdot 1)\\,i+(1-2\\cdot 1)\\,j=2\\,i-j \\\\ ∇f(1,3) & =-2\\cdot (3-2\\cdot 1)\\,i+(3-2\\cdot 1)\\,j=-2\\,i+j \\\\ ∇f(-2,-3) & =-2\\cdot (-3-2\\cdot (-2))\\,i+(-3-2\\cdot (-2))\\,j=-2\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/25/2595c11a1b30fee9d76028010d2c7e608250c0d48ce0191d7da2a5412af6a110.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387271 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387271 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387271 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387271 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387271 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387271 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387271 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387271 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186387275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387271 #edb/